# A complete, production-shaped environment, made of values that are not
# secret. CI sources this file and runs `pnpm check:security` against it twice:
# once as it stands, which must PASS, and once with ALLOWED_EMAILS removed,
# which must FAIL.
#
# Both halves matter. A boot assertion that only ever gets refused
# environments is one nobody has proved will let a correct one through, and an
# assertion that has drifted into accepting anything would pass the first check
# and fail the second.
#
# Every value below is deliberately valid in *shape* and worthless in fact:
# 32 characters of the word "not", sixteen hex digits in order, a base64 key
# that decodes to the sentence "not-a-real-key--not-a-real-key32", and - for
# the Plaid and cron values, which have no shape to satisfy at all - the word
# "notareal" followed by what the variable is and a run of zeroes.
#
# What keeps that honest is two protections that do not depend on each other,
# and neither of them is an exemption for this file. .gitleaks.toml allows
# exactly two *exact value literals* - the client id and the encryption key -
# and no path at all, so anything else secret-shaped pasted into this file is
# still a finding, the same as anywhere else in the repository. (A `paths`
# allow list would make gitleaks skip the whole file before matching, which is
# why there is none; the reasoning is recorded in .gitleaks.toml.) And
# apps/web/test/ci-fixture.test.ts pins every value here by name, so changing
# one fails a test whether or not gitleaks would have recognised what it
# changed into.
#
#   set -a; . ./ci/env.production.fixture; set +a

# Not read by the assertion; present so the file is a whole environment.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://budget_bot:budget_bot@127.0.0.1:5432/budget_bot

# At least 32 characters, or the assertion refuses to boot.
AUTH_SECRET=notnotnotnotnotnotnotnotnotnotnot

AUTH_GITHUB_ID=Iv1.0123456789abcdef
AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=notarealgithuboauthclientsecret0

# The check with something on it, so the passing run is a real pass. CI removes
# this line's variable for the run that has to fail.
ALLOWED_EMAILS=ci@example.com

# Exactly 32 bytes once decoded, which is what the assertion measures.
BANK_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=bm90LWEtcmVhbC1rZXktLW5vdC1hLXJlYWwta2V5MzI=

# `production`, because `sandbox` is refused in production: Sandbox invents
# transactions, and a deployment showing invented money to its owner is worse
# than one that will not boot. So the complete-environment fixture has to be
# the live-Plaid one, which drags in the client id, the secret and the cron
# secret below - all three of which the assertion now insists on together.
PLAID_ENV=production
PLAID_CLIENT_ID=notarealplaidclientid0000
PLAID_SECRET=notarealplaidsecret00000000000

# Required because PLAID_ENV is production: it means the scheduled sync
# endpoint is live and has to be behind a shared secret.
CRON_SECRET=notarealcronsecret000000000000000

# Named on purpose. The test-only sign-in door must be absent in production,
# and a fixture that simply forgot it would not show that.
E2E=0
